WEBVTT
NOTE how-do-scientists-know-how-old-something-is - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.670
A shell from the deep ocean floor reads as
eleven thousand years old by carbon-14

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:06.160
dating.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.236
The same sample, placed in a different lab
using a different isotope system, returns

00:00:11.236 --> 00:00:13.291
eleven thousand two hundred years.

00:00:13.291 --> 00:00:16.994
The methods agree within their stated
uncertainty ranges.

00:00:16.994 --> 00:00:21.541
That convergence is not luck. It is the
structure of how age is known.

00:00:21.541 --> 00:00:24.363
How do scientists know how old something
is?

00:00:24.363 --> 00:00:28.901
You cannot see age. You see a rock, a
bone, a layer of sediment.

00:00:28.901 --> 00:00:33.323
Age is what you calculate after measuring
something that changes at a known rate.

00:00:33.323 --> 00:00:38.433
The measurement might be the ratio of two
isotopes, the number of rings in a coral

00:00:38.433 --> 00:00:41.300
skeleton, or the thickness of sediment
layers.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:44.541
The calculation converts that measurement
into time.

00:00:44.541 --> 00:00:48.792
The method you choose depends on what you
are dating and how old you think it is.

00:00:48.792 --> 00:00:53.117
Carbon-14 works for organic material up to
about fifty thousand years old.

00:00:53.117 --> 00:00:57.809
Uranium-lead dating works for rocks
millions to billions of years old.

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:02.233
Counting layers works when layers form
annually and remain intact.

00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:04.579
No single method covers everything.

00:01:04.579 --> 00:01:08.240
Radiometric dating relies on radioactive
decay.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:14.706
An unstable isotope — the parent — breaks
down into a stable isotope — the daughter

00:01:14.706 --> 00:01:17.277
— at a rate that does not change.

00:01:17.277 --> 00:01:22.339
That rate is the half-life: the time it
takes for half of the parent atoms to

00:01:22.339 --> 00:01:27.144
Carbon-14 has a half-life of five thousand
seven hundred and thirty years.

00:01:27.144 --> 00:01:31.551
It forms in the atmosphere when cosmic
rays strike nitrogen.

00:01:31.551 --> 00:01:35.664
Living organisms take in carbon-14 while
they are alive.

00:01:35.664 --> 00:01:40.470
When they die, they stop taking it in, and
the carbon-14 already in their tissues

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:40.885
decays.

00:01:40.885 --> 00:01:46.642
Measure the ratio of carbon-14 to stable
carbon-12, and you can calculate how long

00:01:46.642 --> 00:01:48.187
ago the organism died.

00:01:48.187 --> 00:01:55.819
Uranium-238 decays to lead-206 with a
half-life of four point five billion

00:01:55.819 --> 00:02:01.915
Potassium-40 decays to argon-40 with a
half-life of one point three billion

00:02:01.915 --> 00:02:07.586
These long half-lives make them useful for
dating rocks and minerals far older than

00:02:07.586 --> 00:02:09.568
anything carbon-14 can reach.

00:02:09.568 --> 00:02:13.712
The decay rate is not affected by
temperature, pressure, or chemical

00:02:13.712 --> 00:02:16.329
That constancy is what makes the method
work.

00:02:16.329 --> 00:02:20.866
You measure the ratio now, apply the known
rate, and calculate backward to the

00:02:20.866 --> 00:02:21.739
starting point.

00:02:21.739 --> 00:02:27.536
Some materials form layers at regular
intervals. Trees add one ring per year.

00:02:27.536 --> 00:02:32.571
Ice sheets in Greenland and Antarctica
trap a new layer of snow each year, and

00:02:32.571 --> 00:02:37.477
that snow compresses into ice while
preserving a record of the atmosphere at

00:02:37.477 --> 00:02:38.574
the time it fell.

00:02:38.574 --> 00:02:43.480
Deep-sea sediments accumulate in layers,
though the rate varies by location.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:48.243
Counting layers gives you an age in years
without needing to measure isotopes.

00:02:48.243 --> 00:02:51.797
The method is direct. The limit is
preservation.

00:02:51.797 --> 00:02:54.907
Layers must remain distinct and countable.

00:02:54.907 --> 00:02:59.128
Mixing, melting, or compression can erase
the boundaries.

00:02:59.128 --> 00:03:03.523
Ice cores from Greenland reach back more
than one hundred thousand years with

00:03:03.523 --> 00:03:06.035
annual layers visible for much of that
span.

00:03:06.035 --> 00:03:11.019
The EPICA ice core from Antarctica,
drilled at Dome C, reaches eight hundred

00:03:11.019 --> 00:03:12.003
thousand years.

00:03:12.003 --> 00:03:16.961
The oldest ice, from Allan Hills in
Antarctica, exceeds one million years,

00:03:16.961 --> 00:03:20.915
though annual layers are no longer
countable at that depth.

00:03:20.915 --> 00:03:26.086
Age at that point comes from modeling ice
flow and comparing to radiometric dates

00:03:26.086 --> 00:03:27.683
from volcanic ash layers.

00:03:27.683 --> 00:03:30.124
Every dating method rests on assumptions.

00:03:30.124 --> 00:03:34.948
For radiometric dating, you assume the
decay rate has not changed, the sample has

00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:39.652
not gained or lost parent or daughter
isotopes since it formed, and you know or

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.736
can measure the initial conditions.

00:03:41.736 --> 00:03:43.570
These assumptions are testable.

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:47.890
Decay rates have been measured in the lab
under extreme conditions — high

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:52.387
temperature, high pressure, strong
magnetic fields — and they do not change.

00:03:52.387 --> 00:03:57.346
Samples can be checked for contamination
by looking for isotopes that should not be

00:03:57.346 --> 00:03:57.704
there.

00:03:57.704 --> 00:04:02.573
Initial conditions can be estimated by
measuring multiple isotopes in the same

00:04:02.573 --> 00:04:07.192
sample or by dating minerals that formed
with no daughter isotope present.

00:04:07.192 --> 00:04:11.898
Zircon crystals, for example, incorporate
uranium but reject lead when they

00:04:11.898 --> 00:04:12.651
crystallize.

00:04:12.651 --> 00:04:18.704
Any lead-206 in a zircon crystal must have
come from uranium-238 decay.

00:04:18.704 --> 00:04:23.924
That makes zircon ideal for uranium-lead
dating because the initial condition —

00:04:23.924 --> 00:04:25.312
zero lead — is known.

00:04:25.312 --> 00:04:28.282
When assumptions are violated, methods
disagree.

00:04:28.282 --> 00:04:33.294
A contaminated sample gives a different
age depending on which isotope system you

00:04:33.294 --> 00:04:33.541
use.

00:04:33.541 --> 00:04:36.419
That disagreement is a signal, not a
failure.

00:04:36.419 --> 00:04:40.000
It tells you the sample is not suitable
for that method.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.363
I'm not anti-science.

00:04:41.363 --> 00:04:46.295
I'm against using science as a magic
authority word that means nobody has to

00:04:46.295 --> 00:04:47.333
show their work.

00:04:47.333 --> 00:04:50.636
Different methods applied to the same
material should agree.

00:04:50.636 --> 00:04:53.664
When they do not, you learn where a method
breaks down.

00:04:53.664 --> 00:04:58.981
Carbon-14 dating assumes the ratio of
carbon-14 to carbon-12 in the atmosphere

00:04:58.981 --> 00:05:00.208
has been constant.

00:05:00.208 --> 00:05:00.909
It has not.

00:05:00.909 --> 00:05:05.880
Tree rings provide a countable record
going back more than ten thousand years,

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.003
and wood from those rings can be carbon-14
dated.

00:05:09.003 --> 00:05:14.090
The comparison shows that atmospheric
carbon-14 levels have varied by up to ten

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:16.731
percent over the past few thousand years.

00:05:16.731 --> 00:05:22.976
That variation is now part of the
calibration curve used to correct

00:05:22.976 --> 00:05:27.669
Volcanic ash layers can be dated by
potassium-argon and also placed in a

00:05:27.669 --> 00:05:31.059
sequence by their position in sediment or
ice cores.

00:05:31.059 --> 00:05:34.742
The radiometric age should match the
layer-counted age.

00:05:34.742 --> 00:05:40.099
When it does, both methods are confirmed.
When it does not, you investigate why.

00:05:40.099 --> 00:05:44.616
Magnetic reversals — times when Earth's
magnetic field flipped north to south —

00:05:44.616 --> 00:05:46.675
are recorded in rocks and sediments.

00:05:46.675 --> 00:05:51.934
The timing of those reversals has been
dated radiometrically and also counted in

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:52.920
sediment cores.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:58.067
The two records agree. That agreement
extends the confidence in both methods.

00:05:58.067 --> 00:06:03.992
Deep-sea sediments form from the slow rain
of dead plankton, dust, and volcanic ash.

00:06:03.992 --> 00:06:05.816
The rate depends on location.

00:06:05.816 --> 00:06:11.037
Abyssal plains far from land accumulate
one to five millimeters per thousand

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:16.744
Continental slopes, closer to sediment
sources, accumulate ten to one hundred

00:06:16.744 --> 00:06:19.042
millimeters per thousand years.

00:06:19.042 --> 00:06:24.675
Near hydrothermal vents, mineral
precipitation can add sediment much

00:06:24.675 --> 00:06:29.140
These rates are measured by dating
sediment cores with multiple methods.

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:31.496
Radiocarbon dates the youngest layers.

00:06:31.496 --> 00:06:36.819
Oxygen isotope ratios in foraminifera
shells track glacial cycles, which have

00:06:36.819 --> 00:06:38.755
known timing from ice cores.

00:06:38.755 --> 00:06:42.286
Magnetic reversals provide tie points at
known ages.

00:06:42.286 --> 00:06:47.176
Microfossils indicate the age range based
on when those species existed.

00:06:47.176 --> 00:06:53.041
The uneven accumulation means you cannot
simply measure depth and divide by an

00:06:53.041 --> 00:06:54.019
average rate.

00:06:54.019 --> 00:06:56.167
Each core must be dated directly.

00:06:56.167 --> 00:07:00.595
The methods used depend on the age range
and the material available.

00:07:00.595 --> 00:07:05.133
Every age measurement comes with
uncertainty. That uncertainty is not a

00:07:05.133 --> 00:07:09.256
It is a statement of what the measurement
can and cannot resolve.

00:07:09.256 --> 00:07:15.134
A radiocarbon date might be reported as
eleven thousand two hundred years plus or

00:07:15.134 --> 00:07:19.416
minus four hundred years at ninety-five
percent confidence.

00:07:19.416 --> 00:07:24.756
That means if you repeated the measurement
many times, ninety-five percent of the

00:07:24.756 --> 00:07:29.833
results would fall between ten thousand
eight hundred and eleven thousand six

00:07:29.833 --> 00:07:30.756
hundred years.

00:07:30.756 --> 00:07:35.569
The uncertainty comes from counting
statistics, instrument precision, and

00:07:35.569 --> 00:07:36.360
sample size.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:41.590
Longer-lived isotopes have larger absolute
uncertainties but smaller relative

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:42.540
uncertainties.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:48.042
A uranium-lead date of one billion years
might have an uncertainty of ten million

00:07:48.042 --> 00:07:49.400
years — one percent.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:54.280
That is precise enough to distinguish rock
formations separated by tens of millions

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:58.749
of years but not precise enough to resolve
events a few million years apart.

00:07:58.749 --> 00:08:02.057
Reporting uncertainty lets you compare
measurements.

00:08:02.057 --> 00:08:06.445
Two ages that overlap within their
uncertainty ranges are consistent.

00:08:06.445 --> 00:08:11.028
Two ages that do not overlap are genuinely
different, and that difference means

00:08:11.028 --> 00:08:13.523
something about the samples or the
methods.

00:08:13.523 --> 00:08:17.649
The visual directives in this script
emphasize process over result.

00:08:17.649 --> 00:08:22.392
The chain from observation to age shows
that age is not a direct measurement.

00:08:22.392 --> 00:08:27.931
The steps directive for radiometric dating
shows that the method is a procedure, not

00:08:27.931 --> 00:08:28.723
a black box.

00:08:28.723 --> 00:08:33.059
The checklist of assumptions shows that
the method is conditional, not absolute.

00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:37.702
The ladder of half-lives shows the range
of timescales different isotopes can

00:08:37.702 --> 00:08:38.184
measure.

00:08:38.184 --> 00:08:41.873
The contrast directive shows that age is
calculated, not observed.

00:08:41.873 --> 00:08:46.511
The sources directive shows that multiple
independent lines of evidence support the

00:08:46.511 --> 00:08:47.405
same conclusion.

00:08:47.405 --> 00:08:51.545
These methods tell you when something
formed or died, not what happened to it

00:08:51.545 --> 00:08:52.083
afterward.

00:08:52.083 --> 00:08:56.379
A rock dated to two billion years old
formed two billion years ago.

00:08:56.379 --> 00:09:01.189
That date does not tell you when the rock
reached the surface, when it was buried,

00:09:01.189 --> 00:09:02.656
or when it was collected.

00:09:02.656 --> 00:09:06.939
Carbon-14 becomes undetectable beyond
about fifty thousand years.

00:09:06.939 --> 00:09:10.629
Material older than that cannot be dated
by this method.

00:09:10.629 --> 00:09:15.628
Uranium-lead dating requires minerals that
incorporate uranium, which limits the

00:09:15.628 --> 00:09:17.440
types of samples it can date.

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:22.605
Layer counting requires continuous,
undisturbed deposition, which is rare over

00:09:22.605 --> 00:09:23.664
long timescales.

00:09:23.664 --> 00:09:26.906
The methods converge where their ranges
overlap.

00:09:26.906 --> 00:09:31.296
That convergence is the strongest evidence
that the methods work.

00:09:31.296 --> 00:09:36.208
Where they do not overlap, you are
extrapolating, and the uncertainty grows.

00:09:36.208 --> 00:09:39.131
Age is not a property. It is a
calculation.

00:09:39.131 --> 00:09:44.501
The calculation depends on a measurement,
a known rate, and stated assumptions.

00:09:44.501 --> 00:09:47.564
The methods are independent, and they
check each other.

00:09:47.564 --> 00:09:49.457
When they agree, the age is known.

00:09:49.457 --> 00:09:52.464
When they disagree, you learn where the
method breaks.
